# How git treats the bytes in this tree.
#
# Declared here rather than inherited from whatever core.autocrlf a contributor
# happens to have, so the same commit produces the same blob on every machine.
# docs/text-and-line-endings.md is where the reasoning lives; this file is the
# declaration and the check derives its exemptions from it rather than carrying
# a list of its own.

# Default. Git decides what is text, and every text path is stored and checked
# out with LF. Nothing here is stored with a carriage return unless a line
# below declares it and says why.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixture bytes are exact.
#
# A fixture exists to prove what a parser does with one specific byte sequence.
# Normalisation on the way into git would silently delete the carriage return
# that a fixture was written to carry, and the test would then pass for a
# reason nobody chose. Declared binary so no byte is ever rewritten, and marked
# -diff because a diff of exact bytes pretends to be readable and is not.
#
# The directory does not exist yet. It is declared before the first fixture
# lands rather than after, because a fixture committed under the default rule
# above has already been normalised by the time anybody notices.
tests/fixtures/** -text -diff

# Formats whose content is binary. Declared rather than left to git's guess,
# because the guess reads the first bytes of the file and a small or unusual
# file can be guessed wrong.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.heic binary
*.heif binary
*.dng binary
*.cr2 binary
*.cr3 binary
*.nef binary
*.arw binary
*.raf binary
*.orf binary
*.rw2 binary
*.icc binary
*.icm binary
*.tflite binary
*.onnx binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary

# Carriage-return exceptions.
#
# A path declared `eol=crlf` here is exempt from the carriage-return check, and
# the check reads that declaration from git rather than holding its own list,
# so the two cannot drift apart. Every such line carries the reason it exists
# on the line above it.
#
# There are none today. The command that shows it:
#
#     git ls-files | git check-attr --stdin eol | grep ': eol: crlf$'
